  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6$ has one of its roots in the interval $(0,3),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of\u00a0}6}{\\text{factor of\u00a0}1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 6\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,3),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\u2193$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=1,-2,3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n1-2+3=2.\n$$",
